# Frozen RP3 model files: their SHA-256 is recorded in freeze_manifest.json over the
# exact bytes. Any line-ending conversion at checkout would silently break every hash
# verification on a Windows clone, so git must never touch them.
artifacts/rp3/frozen/*.txt -text
artifacts/rp3/frozen/freeze_manifest.json -text

# Shell scripts and the reconstructed Supabase SQL are LF everywhere: bash refuses
# CRLF scripts (the publish_mirror.sh audit finding), and the migration tripwire
# hashes SQL bodies against the md5 Supabase reports for the applied LF text.
*.sh text eol=lf
# The reconstructed applied migrations are VERBATIM remote bytes (all LF), and
# the tripwire hashes them against the remote md5. They are byte-exact records,
# not text for git to ever convert in either direction on any platform: -text,
# same regime as the frozen RP3 artifacts above. (With autocrlf=true a plain
# `text` classification would smudge them to CRLF on Windows checkouts.)
supabase/migrations/*.sql -text
supabase/drafts/*.sql -text
supabase/migrations_pending/*.sql -text
